#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34299c34-b199-5100-8fb6-c190eb1db2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square Is Isolated"}] :question/id #uuid "fc7c982c-7e5d-5187-9779-6952f32fe2bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-15019" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression\n$\\left(x^2 + 7x + \\dfrac{49}{4}\\right) + 3$\nas the sum of a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}x + a^2 = (x + {\\color{blue}{a}})^2\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 7x + \\frac{49}{4} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 7x + (\\frac{7}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x + (\\frac{7}{2})^{2} &=  \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{7}{2})^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x^{2} + 7x + \\frac{49}{4}) + 3 &=  \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{7}{2})^{2} + 3 \\mid\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183906 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/id #uuid "05109436-7f8b-5ef9-a460-b53497a138f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-15020" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 + 7x - 1$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nwriting the constant term ${\\color{purple}{-1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 + {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x + {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\dfrac{53}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183907 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/id #uuid "2308b4bc-c09f-5f4c-b879-3bb67c92f885" :question/math-academy-id "e-15021" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression $x^2 - 7x$." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract*\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac{7}{2}}}\\right)^2 - \\dfrac{49}{4}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250313 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34299c34-b199-5100-8fb6-c190eb1db2f7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square Is Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18882416-a042-54ae-8529-beef8e6001a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250315} {:answer/id #uuid "5a896dbe-ffc8-5334-9ea1-3cd576c7d6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+1)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250316} {:answer/id #uuid "a3f38c9b-b51f-5c04-9d72-ad56ea649a11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250317} {:answer/id #uuid "86479541-6531-5770-bcaf-b350b86b0891" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250318} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb1bb6f-38ad-5343-a0c6-8523e83801d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeb1bb6f-38ad-5343-a0c6-8523e83801d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250319} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c8de47a-4432-50f0-86fc-6e44df0942a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250314}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fedd0dc2-da2b-59c1-ac72-8eb5fe3af97b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140299" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression \n\n$$\n({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4})-1\n$$\n\nas the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & = \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-x+\\frac{1}{4})-1 & = \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-1 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12f13d37-e7e5-535d-bc7e-0b922a099d20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250322} {:answer/id #uuid "996b0042-15e7-5522-a6b5-f1bb7262e83b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{11}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250323} {:answer/id #uuid "1377bf28-339b-52aa-8f98-67216edf94d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{45}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250324} {:answer/id #uuid "19fc12f6-8c8e-5f41-85c8-0bb0bff17c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250325} {:answer/id #uuid "977e1574-303f-5c92-aeb3-b807ebdfb28c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19fc12f6-8c8e-5f41-85c8-0bb0bff17c82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250325} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f764c6-e858-5699-aaa5-9c437e4a4942" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250321}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85c3560f-24b7-5e7c-ad52-b0702f53b2de" :question/math-academy-id "q-140310" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression ${x}^{2}-7x+\\frac{5}{4}$ as the sum of the perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{5}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-11.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250327 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54627ecc-a694-500b-a1cd-8304ea781706" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250329} {:answer/id #uuid "74fcd920-09f9-56a4-9538-f185bd5ca6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250330} {:answer/id #uuid "289a6c6e-f5eb-5385-934e-fb5652e72c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250331} {:answer/id #uuid "9c298141-63e1-53e6-a485-502835d41be5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250332} {:answer/id #uuid "577c05ad-e6af-55a7-9e38-efc83e4c7b49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250333}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74fcd920-09f9-56a4-9538-f185bd5ca6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250330} :answer-field/id #uuid "c51d5190-e209-56d1-b41a-b2844562f7bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250328}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7b2d848e-670f-5d12-85f4-c095659a1cd3" :question/math-academy-id "q-140312" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression ${x}^{2}-7x+8$ as the sum of the perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+8\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{17}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250334 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5faf6f99-6cd3-5447-b972-f7282842bde3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250336} {:answer/id #uuid "745fd517-3df0-5024-b9c1-52a5fc1abc43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250337} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c6560f-b288-573b-aae3-e76bb6550eec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250338} {:answer/id #uuid "82d1b080-1647-506a-bbda-4039b3fc03f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250339} {:answer/id #uuid "d791fb73-f0e8-59a7-963d-ca98ed6391a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250340}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82d1b080-1647-506a-bbda-4039b3fc03f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250339} :answer-field/id #uuid "c40d222b-4d99-512e-9065-977d61bdee8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250335}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63fd53ae-9a8f-5b42-8b1d-04b912a748dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-141522" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}+x-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $-2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ec6076ff-1538-5bc0-973d-33d908b0b4d2" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd5b4b70-a36e-5cec-b9a0-1038ac214334" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{4})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250343} {:answer/id #uuid "401105ee-6160-5d76-bbc1-c1f65abb0450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{25}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250344} {:answer/id #uuid "2e99af01-e9da-55af-bcb9-ed3939739ffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(y+\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250345} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4bc4ab-5b42-5785-af1a-073479f8267f" :answe