c{121}{4})-10 & = \\\\ {(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-10 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30e3840b-49fe-5190-9876-0ab93d9561fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250400} {:answer/id #uuid "19b01f82-5fc1-5ae3-8851-128551509fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19b01f82-5fc1-5ae3-8851-128551509fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250401} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f1737ff-5588-5007-918a-2d0e56d3de02" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250399} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71bdbfaa-dfe1-5dd9-8785-f5d90e7b6c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71bdbfaa-dfe1-5dd9-8785-f5d90e7b6c97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250403} :answer-field/id #uuid "73cdd0d5-7b62-5dc8-92ac-bb9552ce8753" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250402}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "892b678d-6349-50af-a4e9-7077b5f4640e" :question/math-academy-id "q-223639" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}-11x+30.$\n\n${x}^{2}-11x+30=(x\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}-$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2}x+{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1481d4a2-64b4-590a-9dbb-c39d3364bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Expressions Containing a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "709755b5-cd2b-56a1-b548-f54396df0834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "709755b5-cd2b-56a1-b548-f54396df0834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250406} :answer-field/id #uuid "27409619-1c4c-547a-a8db-5f57950d66dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250405} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a633aee-8418-579d-ae5b-d82b5d646793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a633aee-8418-579d-ae5b-d82b5d646793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250408} :answer-field/id #uuid "68a227c1-8ec6-5bd4-b367-54afd9617554" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250407}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0bbf3c8-ec3e-5225-98b7-fec9edcb4877" :question/math-academy-id "q-223640" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${w}^{2}-3w+3.$\n\n${w}^{2}-3w+3=(w\\,\\,-$\n{{field-1}} $)^{2}+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{w}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}w+3\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $3$ last:\n\n$$\n{{w}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}w+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(w-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(w-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"}]}
